1 0 0 1 68.3622 548.7677 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 60.5 Tm /F1 9.5 Tf 14 TL .228345 Tw (Detecting anomalous nodes in graphs is a fundamental task in network analysis, with real-world relevance in) Tj T* 0 Tw .32739 Tw (fraud detection, spam identification on social platforms, network intrusion detection, and financial transaction) Tj T* 0 Tw .897779 Tw (monitoring. The difficulty arises because graph data is inherently multi-modal \227 each node carries its own) Tj T* 0 Tw .466549 Tw (feature vector, and the topology of the graph encodes additional structural context. Anomalies can therefore) Tj T* 0 Tw (manifest in different ways depending on which of these modalities is affected.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 445.7677 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 88.5 Tm .79036 Tw 14 TL /F1 9.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (The paper identifies three distinct categories of graph anomalies. ) Tj /F2 9.5 Tf (Contextual anomalies) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( are nodes whose) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.117832 Tw (feature vectors differ markedly from their local neighbourhood \227 e.g., a fake account whose textual content is) Tj T* 0 Tw .996513 Tw (inconsistent with the accounts it is connected to. ) Tj /F2 9.5 Tf (Structural anomalies) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( are nodes embedded in unusually) Tj T* 0 Tw .093334 Tw (dense clique-like subgraphs, a pattern associated with coordinated fake-review rings or bot clusters on social) Tj T* 0 Tw 3.145549 Tw (media. ) Tj /F2 9.5 Tf (Joint-type anomalies) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( are nodes that can only be flagged by considering both features and) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.278514 Tw (connectivity simultaneously \227 for instance, a phishing email sender that connects across many disparate) Tj T* 0 Tw (communities while having atypical communication patterns.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 398.7677 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 32.5 Tm 1.335074 Tw 14 TL /F1 9.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (The central problem the paper addresses is: ) Tj /F3 9.5 Tf (can a single, unified model reliably detect all three anomaly) Tj T* 0 Tw .179043 Tw (types on real attributed graphs, without requiring labelled training data?) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( This is a non-trivial goal because the) Tj T* 0 Tw (three types place conflicting demands on any detection algorithm.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 370.7677 cm
q
.101961 .101961 .180392 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 7 Tm /F2 11 Tf 18 TL (2. Previous Approaches and Their Limitations) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 338.7677 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 18.5 Tm /F1 9.5 Tf 14 TL .579845 Tw (Prior work on graph anomaly detection can be broadly bucketed into three families, each with characteristic) Tj T* 0 Tw (blind spots.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 277.7677 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 46.5 Tm 2.751473 Tw 14 TL /F2 9.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Structure-only methods) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( such as SCAN and centrality-based approaches work purely with the graph) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.750406 Tw (topology. They are reasonably effective at finding structural anomalies \227 dense subgraphs stand out in) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.967629 Tw (degree distributions and clustering coefficients \227 but they are completely blind to contextual anomalies) Tj T* 0 Tw (because they never look at node features at all. False-alarm rates are also high for joint-type anomalies.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 216.7677 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 46.5 Tm .087745 Tw 14 TL /F2 9.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Feature-only methods) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( like LOF, Isolation Forest, and MLPAE work in the opposite direction: they treat each) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.094136 Tw (node's feature vector in isolation and apply classical outlier detection on top of it. While these methods handle) Tj T* 0 Tw .416848 Tw (contextual anomalies reasonably well, they completely ignore graph connectivity, so structural and joint-type) Tj T* 0 Tw (anomalies go undetected.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 71.76772 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 130.5 Tm 2.432471 Tw 14 TL /F2 9.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Graph Autoencoder \(GAE\) based methods) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( \227 DOMINANT, GCNAE, AnomalyDAE \227 combine GNN) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.037112 Tw (encoders with decoders that try to reconstruct the original adjacency structure. The intuition is that normal) Tj T* 0 Tw .975261 Tw (nodes will have low reconstruction error and anomalies will have high error. This works well for contextual) Tj T* 0 Tw .457335 Tw (and densely-connected structural anomalies. However, the reconstruction objective is a ) Tj /F3 9.5 Tf (proximity-driven link) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.091488 Tw (prediction) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf ( task: the decoder only checks whether edges exist between pairs of nodes. This pushes connected) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.39623 Tw (nodes together in latent space but tells the model nothing about the broader neighbourhood context of a) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.847145 Tw (node. As a result, joint-type anomalies \227 whose oddity lies precisely in the combination of connectivity) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.565312 Tw (pattern and feature distribution \227 are largely missed. There is also a non-trivial aggregation problem: to) Tj T* 0 Tw .576261 Tw (assign an anomaly score to a node from edge-level reconstruction errors, one must decide how to combine) Tj T* 0 Tw (losses across all incident edges, and there is no principled way to do this.) Tj T* ET
Q
